## Proof: The Fundamental Group of the Klein Bottle

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains only "Compute the fundamental group $\pi_1$ of the Klein bottle." No solution, sketch, or answer is embedded. Proceeding.

---

### Setup: CW structure and presentation

Realize the Klein bottle $K$ as the quotient of the unit square $[0,1]^2$ with the identifications
$$(s,0)\sim(s,1),\qquad (0,t)\sim(1,1-t).$$
This gives $K$ a CW structure with:
- one $0$-cell $v$ (all four corners identified),
- two $1$-cells $a$ and $b$, where $a$ is the horizontal edge (bottom = top) and $b$ is the vertical edge (left, identified with right via the twist),
- one $2$-cell attached along the loop $a\,b\,a^{-1}\,b$.

The boundary of the $2$-cell is read off from the edge labels traversed around the square: starting at the bottom-left corner and going around, we traverse $a$ (bottom), $b$ (right side, upward), $a^{-1}$ (top, traversed right-to-left because of the orientation reversal from the identification $(s,0)\sim(s,1)$ with matching orientation), and $b$ (left side, upward — note the left edge is identified with the right edge **with reversed orientation** due to the twist $(0,t)\sim(1,1-t)$, so traversing the left edge upward corresponds to $b$, not $b^{-1}$).

Hence the attaching map spells the word $a\,b\,a^{-1}\,b$.

### Applying the standard theorem

For a CW complex with a single $0$-cell, the fundamental group admits the presentation
$$\pi_1(K) \cong \langle \text{1-cells} \mid \text{attaching words of 2-cells}\rangle.$$

This is the standard consequence of the Seifert–van Kampen theorem applied to the $1$-skeleton (a wedge of circles, with free fundamental group) plus the $2$-cells (each killing the attaching word).

Concretely: the $1$-skeleton $K^{(1)}$ is a wedge $S^1_a\vee S^1_b$, so $\pi_1(K^{(1)})\cong F(a,b)$, the free group on $\{a,b\}$. Attaching the single $2$-cell along $a\,b\,a^{-1}\,b$ quotients by the normal closure of that word. Therefore
$$\pi_1(K)\cong \langle a,b \mid a\,b\,a^{-1}\,b = 1\rangle = \langle a,b \mid a\,b\,a^{-1}= b^{-1}\rangle.$$

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\pi_1(K)\cong \langle a,b \mid a\,b\,a^{-1}\,b\rangle.}$$

Equivalently, $\pi_1(K)$ is the semidirect product $\mathbb{Z}\rtimes\mathbb{Z}$ where the generator $a$ of the first $\mathbb{Z}$ acts on the second $\mathbb{Z}=\langle b\rangle$ by inversion $b\mapsto b^{-1}$:
$$\pi_1(K)\cong \mathbb{Z}\rtimes_{-1}\mathbb{Z}.$$

**Sanity checks.**
- Abelianizing gives $\langle a,b\mid ab a^{-1}b,\; ab=ba\rangle$, i.e. $b^2=1$ in the abelianization, so $H_1(K)\cong \mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}/2$, matching the known homology of the Klein bottle. ✓
- The relation $aba^{-1}=b^{-1}$ is exactly the twist/gluing relation of the Klein bottle, distinguishing it from the torus (where the relation would be $aba^{-1}b^{-1}=1$). ✓
- The presentation is non-abelian (e.g. $ab\neq ba$ in the group), consistent with $K$ being non-orientable. ✓

### PROOF COMPLETE
